Carafe: a system call or a call into a Unix library keeps the registers its caller had, as Windows keeps a
thread's user context while it is in the kernel. A suspended thread inside such a call gives those registers and
stops only when the call returns to the program, as a Windows thread takes its suspension on the way back to user
mode; GetThreadContext and SetThreadContext of the thread itself use them too. Callbacks into the program, APCs
and continued contexts are returns to the program. A call made by the x86 emulator for itself is not one: its
thread stops at the emulator's next consistent point, through the doorbell.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

